1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:04,695
A password that never expires is a door that never changes its lock.

2
00:00:04,715 --> 00:00:08,058
Good administrators decide how long a password lives,

3
00:00:08,078 --> 00:00:10,343
and when a user must pick a new one.

4
00:00:10,813 --> 00:00:15,891
In this video: change passwords and adjust password aging for local user accounts.

5
00:00:20,411 --> 00:00:22,994
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

6
00:00:23,464 --> 00:00:27,646
Change passwords and adjust password aging for local user accounts.

7
00:00:28,116 --> 00:00:31,355
We'll set a password, read the aging rules, change them,

8
00:00:31,375 --> 00:00:33,856
and force a new password at the next login.

9
00:00:40,376 --> 00:00:44,258
Password aging is a set of rules about time. The minimum days:

10
00:00:44,278 --> 00:00:49,230
how long a user must wait before changing the password again. The maximum days:

11
00:00:49,250 --> 00:00:53,447
how long the password lives, then it expires. And the warning days:

12
00:00:53,467 --> 00:00:56,783
how many days before the end the user gets a warning.

13
00:01:07,253 --> 00:01:11,397
Two commands do the work. passwd sets or changes a password.

14
00:01:11,417 --> 00:01:15,560
chage, short for change age, reads and sets the aging rules.

15
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:18,753
In this demo the lab changes nothing silently.

16
00:01:27,273 --> 00:01:30,856
First we create the user alice. Then we set her password.

17
00:01:30,876 --> 00:01:34,965
Normally you type sudo passwd alice and enter the password twice.

18
00:01:34,985 --> 00:01:39,390
Here we send it through a pipe with --stdin, so the video can show it.

19
00:01:39,410 --> 00:01:41,602
That is fine for a lab or a script,

20
00:01:41,622 --> 00:01:45,332
but the password then appears on screen and in the history.

21
00:01:45,352 --> 00:01:48,871
passwd -S shows the status: P means the password is set.

22
00:01:48,891 --> 00:01:51,210
Then the date, and the aging numbers.

23
00:02:04,730 --> 00:02:11,320
chage -l lists the aging rules, in plain words. The password was changed today.

24
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:14,750
It expires: never. The minimum is 0 days.

25
00:02:14,770 --> 00:02:20,774
The maximum is 99999 days, which means never. And the warning is 7 days.

26
00:02:30,294 --> 00:02:36,726
Now we change the rules, one option per command. -M sets the maximum:

27
00:02:36,746 --> 00:02:42,150
90 days. -m sets the minimum: 7 days. -W sets the warning:

28
00:02:42,170 --> 00:02:49,163
14 days. Then chage -l again. The password now expires on February 7, 2027:

29
00:02:49,183 --> 00:02:51,501
90 days after the change.

30
00:03:03,021 --> 00:03:07,626
One more: force a new password. passwd -e expires the password now.

31
00:03:07,646 --> 00:03:12,113
The user must choose a new one at the next login. chage shows it:

32
00:03:12,133 --> 00:03:13,838
password must be changed.

33
00:03:22,358 --> 00:03:25,794
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

34
00:03:27,314 --> 00:03:29,582
We reboot. The rules are still there:

35
00:03:29,602 --> 00:03:33,353
the minimum, the maximum, the warning, and the forced change.

36
00:03:33,373 --> 00:03:37,001
Aging is saved in the shadow file, so it survives a reboot.

37
00:03:44,521 --> 00:03:49,839
Your turn. The lab silently creates the user dave first. Set a password for dave.

38
00:03:49,859 --> 00:03:54,979
Set the minimum to 2 days, the maximum to 60 days, and the warning to 10 days.

39
00:03:54,999 --> 00:03:57,747
Force a password change at the next login.

40
00:03:57,767 --> 00:04:00,778
Run the checker, then reboot and run it again.

41
00:04:09,248 --> 00:04:10,999
Try before you open the solution.

42
00:04:16,519 --> 00:04:20,958
Next up: create, delete, and modify local groups and group memberships.

43
00:04:21,428 --> 00:04:24,330
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
